Based on my review, the section under review (`postgres_cluster_driver.py`) is a low-level backend driver that executes SQL DDL statements. The PR changes parameterized/`Identifier`-based queries to string-formatting (a SQL-injection regression), but that is not an authorization flaw class.

Regarding authorization: the driver functions (`create_postgres_db`, `delete_database`, `delete_user`) perform no ownership/role checks, but they are internal helpers invoked by the REST layer. The REST layer (`management_resources.py`) already enforces the documented authorization model — delete requires the caller to supply the correct `db_pwd` and successfully connect (management_resources.py:71-113), and create is an unauthenticated public operation per the documented API (docs/usage.rst). The PR does not change or remove any authorization check; it only alters how SQL strings are built. No changed/removed line introduces a missing or incorrect authorization check, and no explicit policy rule is violated by the changed lines.

